
TASK 12: VERSION CONTROL SYSTEM — GIT

Lab External Practical Guide | Normal Command Prompt + Visual Studio Code

Aim: To learn local Git operations, GitHub remote repositories, branching and merging, merge-conflict resolution, reset and stash operations, and setting up a simple on-premises Git repository.

Before starting: one-time setup

Step 1: Install Git

Install Git for Windows from https://git-scm.com/download/win. During installation, the default options are usually fine.

Step 2: Check installation

git --version

Step 3: Configure your identity

Use your own name and the email associated with your GitHub account.

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
git config --list

Step 4: Install/open VS Code (optional)

Install Visual Studio Code from https://code.visualstudio.com/. Git support is built into VS Code, but Git must be installed separately.

Important: Replace YOUR-USERNAME, YOUR-EMAIL, and SERVERNAME with your real values. Run commands from the folder mentioned in each task.

Task 12(a): Working locally with Git

Aim: Create a local repository, track a file, commit changes, and view history.

Method A — Normal Command Prompt

1. Create a folder and file

mkdir GitLab
cd GitLab
echo Hello Git > index.txt

2. Initialize a Git repository

git init

3. Check the status

git status

4. Stage the file

git add index.txt

5. Commit the file

git commit -m "First commit"

6. View the commit history

git log --oneline

Method B — Using VS Code

1. Open the folder

Open VS Code → File → Open Folder → select the GitLab folder. If it does not exist, create it first.

2. Create a file

In the Explorer panel, click New File, name it index.txt, type Hello Git, and save with Ctrl+S.

3. Open the integrated terminal

Choose Terminal → New Terminal. The terminal should open in the project folder.

4. Initialize Git

git init

5. Stage and commit

Click the Source Control icon on the left. Under Changes, click the + beside index.txt to stage it. Type First commit in the message box and click Commit. If prompted, confirm the commit.

6. Verify

Use the integrated terminal:

git status
git log --oneline

Task 12(b): Working remotely with GitHub

Aim: Connect a local repository to GitHub, push commits, and clone a repository.

Method A — Normal Command Prompt

1. Create an online repository

Sign in at https://github.com → click + → New repository → name it GitLab. For this exercise, do not initialize it with a README, license, or .gitignore.

2. From inside the local GitLab folder, connect the remote

git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/GitLab.git

3. Push to GitHub

git push -u origin main

4. Clone to another folder

Move to a parent folder first, then run:

cd ..
git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/GitLab.git

Method B — Using VS Code

1. Open your local GitLab folder in VS Code.

2. Add the remote in the terminal

git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/GitLab.git
git branch -M main

3. Push

Run this in the integrated terminal:

git push -u origin main

4. Sign in if asked

Complete the browser sign-in/authorization prompt. Check the repository page on GitHub to confirm the files are uploaded.

5. Clone through VS Code

Press Ctrl+Shift+P → type Git: Clone → choose Clone from URL → paste the repository URL → choose a local folder → click Open when prompted.

Task 12(c): Branching and merging

Aim: Create a separate branch, make a change, and merge it into main.

Method A — Normal Command Prompt

1. Create and switch to a feature branch

git switch -c feature

2. Make a change and commit it

echo Feature added >> index.txt
git add index.txt
git commit -m "Added feature"

3. Return to main and merge

git switch main
git merge feature

4. Check branches

git branch

Method B — Using VS Code

1. Create branch

Click the branch name in the lower-left status bar → select Create new branch → enter feature.

2. Change and commit

Edit index.txt, save it, open Source Control, stage the file with +, enter Added feature, and click Commit.

3. Switch to main

Click the branch name in the lower-left corner and select main.

4. Merge

Use the integrated terminal:

git merge feature

5. Verify

git log --oneline --graph --all

Task 12(d): Resolve a merge conflict

Aim: Create a conflict by changing the same line on two branches, then resolve it.

Method A — Normal Command Prompt

1. Ensure you are on main and create a starter file

git switch main
echo Hello World > conflict.txt
git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "Add conflict file"

2. Change the file on main

echo Hello from main > conflict.txt
git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "Update on main"

3. Create another branch from the earlier commit

Create the branch from the parent commit so it has a different version of the same line:

git switch -c test HEAD~1

4. Change the same line on test

echo Hello from test > conflict.txt
git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "Update on test"

5. Merge test into main

git switch main
git merge test

Git should report a conflict. Open conflict.txt. It may contain markers like these:

<<<<<<< HEAD
Hello from main
=======
Hello from test
>>>>>>> test

6. Resolve

Replace all conflict-marker lines with the final text below, then save the file.

Hello from main and test

7. Complete the merge

git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict"

Method B — Using VS Code

1. Reproduce the conflict

Follow the Command Prompt steps above in VS Code's integrated terminal. This ensures both branches modify the same line.

2. Open the conflicted file

VS Code shows conflict actions such as Accept Current Change, Accept Incoming Change, Accept Both Changes, and Compare Changes.

3. Resolve

Choose an option or edit the file manually. For this exercise, keep the final text: Hello from main and test. Remove all <<<<<<<, =======, and >>>>>>> markers.

4. Save, stage, and commit

Save the file. In Source Control, stage conflict.txt and commit with message Resolve merge conflict. Alternatively run:

git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict"

Task 12(e): Git reset and stash operations

Part A — Reset

Practice reset only in a test repository. The --hard option can discard uncommitted tracked changes.

1. Make a test commit

echo Second version >> index.txt
git add index.txt
git commit -m "Second version"
git log --oneline

2. Soft reset example

Removes the latest commit but keeps its changes staged. Use this in a practice repository:

git reset --soft HEAD~1
git status

3. Mixed reset example

Removes the latest commit and unstages its changes while keeping working-tree changes:

git reset --mixed HEAD~1
git status

4. Hard reset example — caution

Only use this when you are certain you want to discard the latest commit and associated tracked changes. Do not use on important work:

git reset --hard HEAD~1

Part B — Stash

1. Make an uncommitted change

echo Temporary change >> index.txt

2. Save it temporarily

git stash push -m "Temporary work"

3. List saved stashes

git stash list

4. Restore the latest stash

git stash pop

Using VS Code for reset and stash

Stash

Open Source Control → click the ... menu → Stash → Stash (wording can vary by version). To restore, use the ... menu → Stash → Apply or Pop Stash. The integrated terminal commands are the most reliable fallback.

Reset

Use the integrated terminal for the reset commands so you can specify --soft, --mixed, or --hard exactly. Check git status and git log --oneline after each operation.

Task 12(f): Set up Git on on-premises hardware

On-premises means the repository is hosted on a computer/server controlled by the college or organization. The following is a simple shared-folder demonstration; the server folder must be shared on the network and users must have appropriate permissions.

Method A — Server and client Command Prompt

1. On the server computer, create a bare repository

Use a path on the server that is suitable for sharing:

mkdir D:\GitServer
git init --bare D:\GitServer\project.git

2. Share the folder

Share D:\GitServer using Windows folder sharing and grant the client user suitable read/write permissions. Use the actual server name and share name.

3. On the client, create a local project

mkdir LocalProject
cd LocalProject
git init
echo Hello Server > index.txt
git add index.txt
git commit -m "First commit"

4. Add the remote and push

Replace the example path with the real UNC path to the shared repository:

git remote add origin \\SERVERNAME\GitServer\project.git
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

5. Clone on a second client

git clone \\SERVERNAME\GitServer\project.git

Method B — Using VS Code

1. On the server

Open the server folder in VS Code's terminal and run:

git init --bare D:\GitServer\project.git

2. On the client

Open the LocalProject folder in VS Code, then use the terminal commands from steps 3 and 4 above.

3. Verify

Run git remote -v and git log --oneline. A real multi-user server should also have proper authentication, backups, and access controls. For larger teams, a managed Git hosting service on internal hardware may be preferable.

Common problems and fixes

Problem

Fix

'git' is not recognized

Install Git for Windows, close and reopen Command Prompt/VS Code, then run git --version.

Author identity unknown

Run git config --global user.name "Your Name" and git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com".

Remote origin already exists

Run git remote -v. Update it with git remote set-url origin REPOSITORY_URL, or remove and re-add the remote if appropriate.

Push rejected

Check that the remote URL is correct and that you have permission. If the remote has commits not present locally, inspect the history before integrating changes.

No merge conflict appears

The two branches may not have changed the same line. Recreate the exercise from the correct parent commit and edit the same line on both branches.

Shared-folder remote cannot be accessed

Check that the share path is correct, the server is online, and the client has read/write permission.

Viva questions — short answers

What is Git? A distributed version control system.

What is GitHub? An online platform for hosting Git repositories.

What is a repository? A folder/database that tracks project files and history.

What is a commit? A saved snapshot of staged changes.

What is a branch? An independent line of development.

What is merging? Combining changes from one branch into another.

What is a merge conflict? A conflict Git cannot automatically resolve.

What does git stash do? Temporarily saves uncommitted changes.

What does git reset do? Moves the branch pointer and may alter staging or working files.

What is a bare repository? A repository without a checked-out working tree, commonly used as a shared remote.

What does git clone do? Creates a local copy of a repository.

What does git push do? Uploads local commits to a remote repository.

